    Section(
      title: '+ How is the amputation level determined? What do the ankle-brachial index and transcutaneous oxygen pressure values indicate, and what is this patient\'s contralateral limb risk?',
      content:
          '**Amputation Level Determination**:\n\nThe goal is to amputate at the most distal level that will still heal primarily, while providing the best functional outcome for prosthetic use.\n\n**Vascular Assessment Tools**:\n1. **Ankle-Brachial Index (ABI)**:\n- Normal: 0.9-1.3\n- Mild PAD: 0.7-0.89\n- Moderate PAD: 0.4-0.69\n- Severe PAD: <0.4\n- **Right ABI 0.3**: Severe PAD — unlikely to heal at the ankle or foot level. BKA is appropriate.\n- **Left ABI 0.65**: Moderate PAD — contralateral limb at significant risk. BKA heals reliably when ABI >0.5 at the amputation level.\n- **Caveat in diabetes**: ABI may be falsely elevated due to medial arterial calcification (Monckeberg sclerosis). Non-compressible arteries (ABI >1.3) require alternative assessment.\n\n2. **Transcutaneous Oxygen Pressure (TcPO2)**:\n- Measures local tissue oxygenation at the proposed amputation level\n- **>40 mmHg**: High likelihood of primary healing (>90%)\n- **30-40 mmHg**: Moderate healing potential\n- **<30 mmHg**: Poor healing potential — consider more proximal level\n- Should be measured at the proposed BKA level (anterior tibial, ~12-15 cm below tibial tubercle)\n\n3. **Additional Studies**:\n- **Segmental pressures**: Cuff pressures at thigh, calf, and ankle levels to identify the level of stenosis/occlusion\n- **Skin perfusion pressure (SPP)**: >30 mmHg at the proposed level predicts healing\n- **CT angiography/MR angiography**: Define vascular anatomy if revascularization is being considered\n\n**BKA Surgical Considerations**:\n- Standard BKA preserves 12-15 cm of tibia from the tibial tubercle (ideal for prosthetic fitting)\n- Posterior myocutaneous flap technique (long posterior flap) — provides well-padded, well-vascularized residual limb\n- Bone should be beveled anteriorly to prevent anterior tibial pressure on prosthetic socket\n\n**Contralateral Limb Risk — This Is a Critical Issue**:\n- **5-year contralateral amputation rate** in dysvascular amputees: **30-50%**\n- This patient\'s left foot has **ALL major risk factors**: neuropathy, PAD, prior ulcer, deformity, poor glycemic control\n- **Risk Category 3** per International Working Group on the Diabetic Foot: History of ulcer or amputation — highest risk category\n- Aggressive contralateral foot preservation is arguably AS important as the amputation rehabilitation itself',
