    Section(
      title: '+ Design a comprehensive post-amputation rehabilitation plan including prosthetic candidacy assessment, diabetic foot care for the remaining limb, and phantom pain management.',
      content:
          '**Post-Amputation Rehabilitation Plan**:\n\n**1. Residual Limb Management**:\n- Rigid removable dressing (RRD) for edema control and limb protection\n- Transition to elastic shrinker sock when wound is fully healed\n- Wound assessment daily; monitor for signs of infection or dehiscence\n- Desensitization program: tapping, massage, texture exposure progression\n- Avoid pillow under the knee (hip/knee flexion contracture prevention)\n\n**2. Prosthetic Candidacy Assessment**:\n- **K-Level Determination** (Medicare Functional Classification):\n  - K0: Does not ambulate\n  - K1: Limited household ambulator\n  - K2: Limited community ambulator\n  - K3: Full community ambulator\n  - K4: Active adult/athlete\n  - **This patient: Estimated K2** (limited community ambulator) given age, comorbidities (COPD, CAD, PAD, obesity), deconditioning, and contralateral limb disease. May progress to K2-3 with rehabilitation.\n- **Positive prognostic factors**: Pre-injury community ambulator, motivated, supportive wife, single-level home\n- **Negative prognostic factors**: Age 62, multiple comorbidities, contralateral PAD/neuropathy, poor nutritional status, obesity, deconditioning\n- **Prosthetic prescription** (for K2 dysvascular BKA):\n  - Socket: PTB (patellar tendon bearing) or TSB (total surface bearing) with gel liner\n  - Suspension: Pin/lock or suction\n  - Foot: SACH (Solid Ankle Cushion Heel) or single-axis foot — appropriate for K2 level; low maintenance, stable\n  - Consider: Dynamic response foot if patient progresses to K3\n\n**3. Therapy Program**:\n- **PT**: Bed mobility, transfer training, pre-gait activities, single-limb standing balance (with appropriate safety for neuropathic left foot), upper extremity and core strengthening, wheelchair mobility, gait training with walker when prosthesis is ready\n- **OT**: ADL training, bathroom equipment use, energy conservation, home modification planning\n- **Prosthetic gait training**: Progressive weight-bearing in parallel bars, walker, then single-point cane. Emphasis on equal step length and weight acceptance on prosthetic side.\n\n**4. Contralateral Limb (Left Foot) Preservation Program**:\n- **Daily foot inspection**: Patient and wife trained to check for redness, blisters, calluses, cracks, temperature changes daily\n- **Proper footwear**: Medicare Therapeutic Shoe Program (diabetic shoe benefit) — custom-molded shoes with accommodative inserts to redistribute pressure from metatarsal heads and hammer toes\n- **Offloading**: Custom insoles with metatarsal pad and total contact design\n- **Skin care**: Moisturize feet daily (not between toes), treat tinea pedis with antifungal cream, podiatry referral for nail care (onychomycosis treatment, safe nail trimming)\n- **Vascular monitoring**: ABI monitoring every 6-12 months; vascular surgery follow-up\n- **Neuropathy management**: Continue gabapentin; proper shoe fitting (no walking barefoot EVER); protective socks\n- **Weight bearing precautions**: The prosthetic side will bear significant load, but the remaining limb will also bear increased load. Monitor for stress fractures, Charcot neuroarthropathy.\n\n**5. Phantom Limb Pain Management**:\n- **Mirror therapy**: 15 minutes daily — place mirror to reflect the intact limb, creating the illusion of two limbs. Perform bilateral movements while watching the mirror. Evidence-based for phantom pain reduction.\n- **Pharmacologic**: Gabapentin (already on 300 mg TID — titrate to 600 mg TID). Add duloxetine for neuropathic pain + mood benefit. Consider pregabalin if gabapentin insufficient.\n- **TENS**: Applied to residual limb proximal to the surgical site.\n- **Graded motor imagery**: Laterality recognition → imagined movements → mirror therapy (progressive approach).\n\n**6. Glycemic Optimization**:\n- Endocrinology consultation for insulin regimen adjustment\n- Target HbA1c <7-8% (individualized for comorbidities and hypoglycemia risk)\n- Dietitian-led diabetes education and meal planning\n- Blood glucose monitoring QID with insulin adjustment protocol',
