    Section(
      title: '+ Describe the prosthetic prescription process, DME authorization pathway, and the Medicare Diabetic Shoe Program.',
      content:
          '**Prosthetic Prescription Process**:\n\n1. **Physician prescription**: The physiatrist writes a detailed prosthetic prescription specifying:\n- Type of prosthesis (right BKA endoskeletal)\n- Socket type and suspension\n- Foot/ankle component\n- K-level determination with functional justification\n- Medical necessity documentation\n\n2. **Certified prosthetist (CP)**: Works with the patient to fabricate and fit the prosthesis. Must hold ABC (American Board for Certification) or BOC (Board of Certification) credentials.\n\n3. **Fitting and fabrication timeline**:\n- Diagnostic/check socket: 6-8 weeks post-amputation (once wound healed and limb volume stabilizing)\n- Preparatory prosthesis: Used for initial training while limb continues to mature\n- Definitive prosthesis: 6-12 months post-amputation when limb volume has stabilized\n- Socket adjustments and replacements as needed\n\n4. **Insurance authorization**:\n- Medicare Part B covers prosthetic devices (L-codes for billing)\n- Prior authorization required for many components\n- Detailed documentation of K-level, functional goals, and medical necessity\n- Replacement prosthesis covered when current device is worn or no longer fits (typically every 3-5 years for socket, 3 years for foot)\n\n**Medicare Therapeutic Shoe Program (Diabetic Shoe Benefit)**:\n- **Eligibility**: Patients with diabetes AND at least one of the following: peripheral neuropathy with callus formation, prior foot ulceration, prior amputation, foot deformity, poor circulation\n- **This patient qualifies** on multiple criteria\n- **Coverage**: 1 pair of custom-molded shoes + 3 pairs of custom inserts per calendar year, OR 1 pair of depth shoes + 3 pairs of inserts per year\n- **Process**:\n  1. **Certifying physician** (PCP or specialist managing diabetes) completes the Statement of Certifying Physician (Medicare form)\n  2. **Prescribing physician** (may be the same) writes the shoe prescription\n  3. **Qualified supplier** (pedorthist, orthotist, or prosthetist) fits and dispenses the shoes\n- **Important**: The certifying physician must document the qualifying condition within the 6 months preceding the shoe prescription\n\n**DME Authorization for Home Equipment**:\n- **Hospital bed**: May be needed if difficulty with transfers from standard bed height\n- **Wheelchair**: Manual wheelchair for use before prosthetic fitting and as backup\n- **Shower bench/transfer bench**: For safe bathing\n- **Grab bars**: Bathroom safety\n- **Ramp**: 3 steps at home entrance — portable ramp for wheelchair access; permanent ramp for long-term\n- **Walker**: Rolling walker for initial prosthetic ambulation training at home\n- All require physician prescription and medical necessity documentation\n- Medicare Part B covers DME at 80% after deductible (patient responsible for 20% copay)',
