    Section(
      title: 'DOMAIN E: INTERPERSONAL AND COMMUNICATION SKILLS',
      content:
          'Two days before surgery, you visit the patient for the pre-operative rehabilitation consultation. He is sitting quietly in his bed, staring out the window. When you introduce yourself, he says with a trembling voice: \'I\'m losing a part of myself. I don\'t know who I am without two legs. My wife says it will be okay, but she doesn\'t understand. Nothing is going to be okay.\'',
    ),
    Section(
      title: '+ How would you counsel this patient who is grieving the anticipated loss of his limb?',
      content:
          '**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Be fully present — do not rush this conversation**:\nPull up a chair. Sit at his level. Maintain eye contact. Do not check your phone or watch. Allow silence after he speaks.\n\n\'I hear you. Losing a part of your body is one of the most difficult things a person can face, and what you are feeling right now — the grief, the fear, the sense of loss — all of that is completely valid. You do not have to pretend to be okay.\'\n\n**2. Name the grief directly**:\n\'What you are experiencing is a form of grief, and it is real. People grieve the loss of a limb in much the same way they grieve other losses — there can be denial, anger, sadness, bargaining, and eventually acceptance. These stages do not happen in order, and they are not linear. Some days will be harder than others, and that is normal.\'\n\n**3. Acknowledge his identity concern**:\n\'You said you do not know who you are without two legs. That tells me that your sense of self — your identity as a strong, capable man — is deeply important to you. I want you to hear this: you are not losing who you are. You are facing a change in your body, but the person you are — a husband, a father, a man who has worked hard his whole life — that does not change with this surgery.\'\n\n**4. Provide honest, hopeful information**:\n\'I am not going to sugarcoat this — the recovery process will be challenging, and there will be hard days. But I want to share something with you: the majority of patients who go through below-knee amputation with proper rehabilitation do walk again. You were walking before this happened, and you have the strength and determination to get back on your feet — literally. Our job is to help you get there.\'\n\n**5. Address the gap with his wife**:\n\'You mentioned that your wife says it will be okay but she does not understand. I suspect she is scared too and is trying to stay strong for you. One of the things that can really help is having an honest conversation together — with me, with a counselor, or just the two of you — where you both can express your fears. I find that couples who face this together, honestly, do better than those who try to protect each other from their feelings.\'\n\n**6. Offer concrete emotional support**:\n\'I would like to do two things before your surgery. First, I am going to have our psychologist come talk with you today — not because something is wrong with you, but because this is an incredibly stressful time and having professional support makes a real difference. Second, I know a patient who went through the same surgery about a year ago. He has volunteered to be available to talk with patients before their amputation. He has walked in your shoes — literally and figuratively. Would you be willing to speak with him?\'\n\n**7. Create a bridge to the future**:\n\'After the surgery, we are going to start working together right away. I will see you the day after your operation, and we will begin the process of getting you stronger and preparing you for a prosthetic leg. I want you to have something to look forward to, not just something to fear. You are not alone in this.\'\n\n**Key principles**: Unhurried presence, naming grief directly, validating identity concerns, honest hope without false reassurance, addressing family dynamics, psychology referral, peer support, and creating a forward-looking narrative.',
